data: {"type":"TEXT_MESSAGE_CONTENT","messageId":"d18828198e176b9dbbb088c415683f84.v1","delta":"**Observed**\n- Mutation-tested cases: 1,000; KRAS-mutated: 480; KRAS not detected: 520.\n- Groups: KRAS not detected (ORION) with 520 RNA-seq-eligible samples (control), and KRAS mutated (ORION) with 480 (case).\n- Method: Wilcoxon rank-sum; genes ranked by adjusted p-value; fold change is log2(KRAS mutated (ORION) / KRAS not detected (ORION)), so a positive value is higher in KRAS mutated (ORION).\n- 16 of 7,684 tested genes passed the viewer's thresholds.\n- Top-ranked genes by adjusted p-value: BRAF (log2FC 1.12, adjusted p 2.1e-9); ETV4 (log2FC 0.94, adjusted p 6.8e-9); JUN (log2FC -0.87, adjusted p 1.4e-8); DUSP6 (log2FC 1.31, adjusted p 3.9e-8); FOS (log2FC -1.02, adjusted p 7.7e-8); TP53 (log2FC -0.66, adjusted p 2.6e-7); MAPK1 (log2FC 2.17, adjusted p 5.0e-7); DIS3 (log2FC 1.88, adjusted p 1.2e-6); ETV5 (log2FC 1.54, adjusted p 3.3e-6); KRAS (log2FC 0.71, adjusted p 8.1e-6).\n\n**Interpretation**\n- In this synthetic dataset, BRAF has the smallest adjusted p-value between the groups, with higher expression in KRAS mutated (ORION) than in KRAS not detected (ORION).\n- Differential expression alone does not establish a mechanism, and these results carry no clinical meaning.\n\n**Hypotheses and next steps**\n- Is BRAF expression also associated with survival in these cohorts?\n\n**Limitations**\n- KRAS status comes from the six-gene mutation panel (KRAS, NRAS, TP53, BRAF, DIS3, MYC); other mutations are not profiled.\n- You chose Don't save, so no cohort was saved: the chart uses the unsaved groups and Open with this cohort is off.\n\n_Synthetic sandbox — all data is fabricated and has no clinical meaning._"}

